# How to use this file
# --------------------
# This is an example func: a named list of ShellCraft commands you run when you need them.
# It isn't loaded while its name ends in .sample. To use it, copy it to a name ending in .func:
#
#     cp example.func.sample recon.func
#
# Then run it with `func run recon example.com`, or just `recon example.com` (see command: yes).
# $1, $2… are the arguments, $@ all of them and $# how many. It stops at the first step that fails.
#
# The header below (the `# key: value` lines) describes the func:
#   func         its name (the file name wins)
#   description  one line, shown by `func list`
#   system       yes if a step runs a program from your PATH (needs `settings system_commands on`)
#   output       show: every step's output prints. quiet: only echo/printf and steps starting with @
#   command      yes: typing its name runs it
#   usage        how to call it
# Every line after the header is one step. Lines starting with # are comments, and so is a note
# after a step with a space before and after the # (queryDns $1   # look up the records).
#
# Funcs here work in every profile; a profile's own go in ~/.shellcraft/profiles/NAME/funcs/.
# More: `man func`, or docs/funcs.md.

# func: recon
# description: Quick look at a domain: its subdomains and DNS records
# system: no
# output: show
# command: yes
# usage: recon DOMAIN
queryCert -s $1 > subs-$1.txt     # subdomains from certificate logs
queryDns $1                       # the DNS records
@ cat subs-$1.txt | wc -l
echo "recon of $1 done, subdomains in subs-$1.txt"
